# Template. Copy to config/op-refs.env (gitignored) and fill in your 1Password
# refs:  cp config/op-refs.env.example config/op-refs.env
#
# op-refs.env maps ENV_VAR = op://vault/item/field. When the gateway spawns a
# host MCP server it resolves those refs from 1Password and injects them as env
# vars — the secret never touches disk or the sandbox. A server that needs no
# credentials needs no entry here at all.
#
# This file holds op://vault/item/field REFERENCES only. A NON-secret value may
# be a plain literal, but only when the active pack declares that variable as
# env_keys on the integration that needs it — pix allowlists nothing itself.
# Everything secret (tokens, keyring passwords) is an op:// ref resolved from
# 1Password at spawn time — never a pasted secret.
#
# Every line below is COMMENTED OUT: a freshly-copied file has zero active
# entries. Uncomment + fill in a line only when you wire that server.
#
# Verify:  op read "op://<vault>/<item>/<field>" >/dev/null && echo OK
# Tip:     1Password app -> right-click a field -> "Copy Secret Reference" gives the
#          exact ref (and disambiguates duplicate item names by ID).

# Your ACTIVE PACK names the variables to add here, and there is nothing to
# seed by default: pix ships no MCP servers, so it cannot know which credentials
# you need. See the pack's own README.
#
#   pix secret set <ENV_VAR> op://<vault>/<item>/<field>
#   pix secret check          # resolves every ref

# A pack's servers add their own refs here (kept out of the public repo).

# Web-search TOOL keys (pi-web-access backends). These are NOT model providers:
# nothing routes to them, `pix models add` does not accept them, and a missing
# one degrades web search instead of blocking a launch. They are seeded,
# checked and mirrored exactly like a model key:
#   pix secret set PARALLEL_API_KEY op://<vault>/<item>/<field>
#   pix secret sync        # mirrors it into the sbx secret store
# The kit injects the value as the x-api-key header on api.parallel.ai, so the
# real key stays on the host and only the proxy sentinel enters the sandbox.
# Get a key at https://platform.parallel.ai
# PARALLEL_API_KEY=op://<vault>/<item>/<field>
